  "worked_solution": "Since $(x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x)$, it must divide into $f(x)$ with no remainder. In other words, when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x+2),$ the remainder is $0.$ It follows from the remainder theorem that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =f(-2) \\\\  & =a(-2)^{2}+b(-2)-10 \\\\  & =a(4)+b(-2)-10 \\\\  & =4a-2b-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, since we're told that the remainder is $18$ when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x+4),$ it follows from the remainder theorem that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}18 & =f(-4) \\\\  & =a(-4)^{2}+b(-4)-10 \\\\  & =a(16)+b(-4)-10 \\\\  & =16a-4b-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0=4a-2b-10 \\\\ 18=16a-4b-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying each equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2a-b=5 \\\\ 4a-b=7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo eliminate $a,$ we subtract the equations and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & a-b &  & =7 \\\\ -\\,2 & a-b &  & =5 \\\\ 2 & a &  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get $2a=2,$ and hence $a=1.$ Finally, we substitute it into $2a-b=5$ to find $b=-3.$ \n\nTherefore, our final solution is $(a,b)=(1,-3).$ \n\nFinally then, $a+b=1-3=-2$",
